A||||5||fierogt88||Decisions, Decisions.  Help me with car audio.||||1||
Z||000000||fierogt88||06-19-2001||03:20 PM||||Since I happened to find a little extra cash I have decided to upgrade the audio system in my gt.  I'm thinking about getting AusFiero's console box, but first things first, I need to know some things before diving in.<P>Topic/Question #1: how do I size my amp and subwoofer?  Do I use the peak watts or RMS or what?  Possibly related: what is the difference in 2 Ohm and 4 Ohm and is this something I have to consider??  With one sub i would guess i will be bridging a 2 channel amp... since i don't have the stock subwoofer option is it possible to get an amp that fits the location of the stock amp?  For pre-amp outputs do i need to watch the voltage (i.e 2.2V or 4V or something) or does this really matter?<P>Topic/Question #2:  What is the difference for sealed/ported/bandpass/etc. and how does it affect sound quality i.e. which one is better for a fiero?<P>Topic/Question #3: Does MP3 sound good?  Is it worth it to get an MP3 head unit?  I've been looking at 2 models, <P>the Sony XPlod CDX-MP450X:<BR>nifty-&gt;matches the fiero guages fairly well<BR>not-so-nifty-&gt;looks like every other sony <P><A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-c3GdEsqz2qZ/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=62700&I=158CDXMP45&o=M&a=0" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-c3GdEsqz2qZ/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=6270 0&I=158CDXMP45&o=M&a=0</A> <P>the AIWA CDC-MP32:<BR>nifty-&gt;steering wheel remote, disappearing faceplate<BR>not-so-nifty-&gt;not sure it matches the fiero interior <P><A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-c3GdEsqz2qZ/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=62700&I=570CDCMP32&o=M&a=0" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-c3GdEsqz2qZ/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=6270 0&I=570CDCMP32&o=M&a=0</A> <P>which do you guys prefer?<P>Any other issues that anybody wants to throw in here are welcome, because I probably am not aware of many stereo issues.  I have installed a few head units to upgrade from tape to CD but that's the extent of my experience with sound systems.<P>TIA<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by fierogt88 (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||saleenfiero||06-19-2001||06:19 PM||||Hey, I'm not gonna answer your other questions because others here can answer them better, but I feel the choice with MP3 is whether you want quality or quantity.  You probably know you can fit a ton of songs with MP3, but unless they are pretty highly sampled, they will not sound as good at the extreme highs and lows as CD's.  I prefer quality and buy the CD's I want instead of Napster and I'm happy that way.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||06-19-2001||06:58 PM||||Just my area of expertise...<P>Depends on what you are looking for. You will use the RMS (root mean square) aka continous power to get the correct amp for your sub. A 4 ohm sub is most common, is has one voice coil. A 2ohm sub usally has dual 4 ohm voice coils, which can be hooked up to make the sub either a 2 ohm or 8 ohm load. If you get a DVC (dual voice coil), you will have to get an amp that is 2ohm mono stable (which means it can run a 2ohm load while bridged), look for that term. Most lower brand/cheap amps are only 4 ohm mono stable. If you hook a 2 ohm sub to an amp that is only 4ohm mono stable, then the amp will overheat, sound bad, and probally fry. If your amp IS capable of 2ohm mono stable, then it will be more efficient on your charging system, make less heat, and put out more power into your sub. <BR>Think of it this way: Your sub is a resistor. Your amp is the power source. If you put a 2 ohm resistor in there, there will be twice the amount of power going thru as would be if you had a 4 ohm resistor. But the power source will get hotter, and be put under more stress. Only higher priced amps can handle that kinda stress and heat. If you have the money, go the 2 ohm route. Understanding that concept?<P>Your CD player is what has the preouts. The higher the better. As a better, more high quality signal will travel thru the RCA's to your amp. Don't worry about overpowering your amp with preout power, it never happens from a CD player. 2.2v is good if your just sending the signal to one amp. If you split and go to 2 or more amps, you need very high preout voltage. I think your only powering your subwoofer amp, so 2.2v should be fine, but higher IS better.<P>The difference between boxes:<BR><B>Sealed</B>: Takes up small amount of space, inside is completly sealed, provides very tight and accurate bass. Only bad things is that the subwoofer will need more power to reach its full potential (aka xMax, excursion). Make sure you get an amp with alot of power if your useing a sealed box. They sound great though!<P><B>Ported</B>: Inside is sealed, except for a port tube of the correct length (determines tuneing frequency), not all subs can be used in a ported box because there is not as much resistance on the cone, so the suspension much be made for ported usage. Ported boxes are LOUD. About 3-5 dB louder then a sealed. To a human ear, 3 dB is precieved as twice as loud. Disadvantage is that they are not as tight as a sealed box. <P><B>Bandpass</B>: Bandpass is pretty much a sealed box and ported box together. The sub fires into the ported box, and the back of the sub is in the sealed box. This enclosure is the loudest, and most efficent. However, the sound quality is just not there. It is all loudness. They only produce bass within a narrow band, for which they can be tuned for. I don't like bandpass much at all, and would not reccomend it to you if you like good sound quality. DO use this if you want to be heard 4 blocks away with very little power going to the sub.<P>MP3 sounds just as good as a CD IMO. I really can't tell the difference in a car. However, the second half of your question: Is it worth it to get an MP3 headunit. I would have to say no! I just bought a new HU, and looked extensivly into the MP3 capable ones. I found that the Kenwood Z828 is about the nicest one out there. I however look for good and convient features in a unit. Apperently, they scrap most of the features found on their other lines to cut down the cost of an MP3 player. Escentially, its a trade off. I ended up getting a Kenwood X-917 because of the features. <P>I'm not crazy about either of those models you mentioned, but I would take the Awia over the Sony.<P>Feel very free to email me with any questions you may have about sound systems or anything related. sean@kdsi.net is my addy, I'm pretty much on the net most of the day, so it won't take long to get back to you.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierogt88||06-19-2001||08:24 PM||||Wow, that cleared up a lot!  Ok, so what features am I losing by going the MP3 route?  Or, rather, what features are there that don't come in the MP3 players that I probably want to have?<P>And if the cd player has two sets of 2.2V preamp outputs, and i send one to my base amp and the other to a smaller amp for the rest of the speakers, is that ok?  or is 4V really that much better?<P>and why is there sand in my EGR vacuum hose?<P>Thanks, btw.  most helpful.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||06-20-2001||12:50 AM||||I think Sean summed it up pretty well.  Take his word for it, a DVC sub with a 4ohm amp isn't pretty.  My friend toasted his $500 amp that way and there is still a burning smell in his trunk that won't go away.<P>I am going with the new Sony MP3 headunit.  I can put every Metallica song I have (which is every single one) on 2 CD's as compared to choosing about 24 for 2 CD's.  The convenience is awesome.  Stick one CD in there and just let it run for 4 hours.  They say 8 hours but thats with lower quality MP3's that don't take up too much space.  If you want CD quality MP3s the most is about 3-4 hours depending on length of the songs.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Seanh||06-20-2001||03:49 PM||||The units I looked into when I decided I was getting tired of looking at my Premier DEH-P900R were the following: <P>Premier DEH-P930R (Premier decks have such awesome amounts of features, I love their features and no brand I'v seen can really out-do them in that department. The display is the Organic EL, which is the same as the one I currently had, and I wanted somthing different to look at, that EL is just too bright. That eliminated it from the running.<P>Kenwood Z919 - Kenwood's first generation MP3 player. Very nice display, and had a fairly nice selection of features. Definatly a possibility.<P>Kenwood Z828 - Kenwood's second generation MP3 player. Has very nice display, but lacked features. Even the older Z919 had it beaten on features. Ooch..$629 though!<P>Kenwood X917 - Kenwood's current top of the line CD player only. Has tons of features like Satalite radio ready, Radio Data System, DMask+, Motirized face plate, full motion display, built in Advanced crossover, very nice EQ. All those features were scrapped on the X828 MP3 player, and those were mostly the kinda things I look for in a good deck. In the end, I bought this deck, ran me $450 and put my $780 Premier to shame! This is an awesome deck. It even has individual speaker tuning, with presets for 4"-5" speakers, and 6-1/2"-up. It sets all the bass, trebal, center, midrange, all that good stuff, so that the speaker can run at its optimum abaility. Seperate front AND rear AND sub crossovers, built in! &lt;--That feature really sold me on it. I love decks with built in crossovers, mainly because I hate all external crossovers.<P>It really depends on what kinda features you are looking for. Most friends of mine have cheap little Pioneer, Jensen, and Sony deck's in their cars, and they love them. Then I get my TOTL Kenwood, and its just amazing the difference in sound quality it puts out. I think you will be happy either MP3 unit you choose, just for the convience of being able to play MP3's in it. I usally get myself a new deck about every year, so I decided to wait until next year to get a MP3 deck, when they are more perfected and include more features. <P>Check out the deck's your interested in on the crutchfield website: <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com" TARGET=_blank>www.crutchfield.com</A>  Once there, just open up 2 browser windows and look at each deck in one window, and compare their basic features. I'll look at the Sony one sometime today and see what I really think of it, but other thier Mobile ES line, Im not too crazy about Sony.<P>BTW, 2.2v x 2 pair of preouts is good. However, chances are those are front and rear pairs of preouts. So, if your going to be running 2 pair of speakers (front and rear) off a 4 channel amp, and then a sub off another amp, thats actually 6 channels. Because, your amp for the speakers is 4 channel, so it will have 4 RCA inputs, of which you will want to run all 4 of your preouts into it to acheive a "fade-able" sound system. Then, your going to be running either the front or rear RCA's to your subwoofer amp also. So, this would be like haveing a 2.2v and 2 1.1v outputs. When you get down to 1.1v, it starts getting noticable. My old Premier I just took out has 6.5v x 3 preouts, and it really awesome. 4v would be much better in your case, but 2.2v could be used. Theres also the option to use a line driver, which increases the output voltage to like 7v, but thats a whole other topic.<P>Sand in your EGR vacuum hose...hmmm...never heard of that happening before. Did you get your car stuck in sand latly? Only way I could think this could happen would be sand getting up, as in backwards, thru your exhaust system, but that would also require a bad EGR valve. Perhaps your EGR silanoid (spelling & english were not good subjects for me) is bad. ||||reg||1
